11264 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The countless apartment complexes that fill Seoul\047s skyline are more than just clusters of homes\056 They are a vast barometer of where capital flows and where it pools in Korean society\056 Among them\054 the apartments of Gangnam \024 built south of the Han River \024 have long functioned as more than a physical administrative district\056 They represent the apex of Korean capitalism\072 the last line of defense against the erosion of monetary value\056 The problem is that this fortress is being sealed ever more tightly\056 Even households earning 200 million won \050approximately \044145\054000 USD\051 a year \024 the top one percent of earners in South Korea \024 must save every penny for thirty years before they can seriously consider buying a newly built apartment unit in Gangnam\056i\n\nThis report examines tokenization as a technological solution capable of cracking open this increasingly closed asset structure\056 It takes a comprehensive look at how breaking a 10 billion won \050roughly \0447\0562 million USD\051 apartment unit into digital shares worth one million won \050about \044720\051 each actually works in practice\073 what legal and tax structures are required across three realistic scenarios\073 and how the skeptics who say the law will never allow it can be answered\056 To state the conclusion upfront\072 the technology is already ready\056 The market\054 too\054 is moving toward acceptance\056 Given that combination\054 the case for tokenization is increasingly a matter of when rather than if\056)
     /K [ 1 ]
     /P 11265 0 R
     /Pg 1372 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

