11363 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Consider a retired homeowner \024 call him Mr\056 A \024 who owns an apartment unit in Gangnam worth 10 billion won \050approximately \0447\0562 million USD\051\056 He is asset\055rich but cash\055poor\072 substantial wealth on paper\054 but insufficient disposable income for comfortable daily life\056 Rather than taking out a loan\054 Mr\056 A tokenizes 30 percent of his apartment\047s equity and sells those tokens on the market\056 He keeps his home and his right to live in it\054 while immediately unlocking 3 billion won \050roughly \0442\0562 million USD\051 in cash\056 Instead of borrowing money and paying interest to a bank\054 he gains a silent partner and distributes his risk\056 The structure preserves household balance sheet health while generating liquidity \024 a meaningful alternative to the debt\055heavy models that dominate today\056\n\nThat said\054 the structure is not without friction\056 When ownership\054 occupancy\054 and investment stake sit in different hands\054 decision\055making misalignments are bound to emerge \024 and must be carefully designed around\056 If the apartment     \052)
