11372 0 obj
  << /ActualText (appreciates from 10 billion to 20 billion won\054 token holders eager to realize their gains may find themselves at odds with an owner who simply wants to stay in his home\056 Two mechanisms address this\056 The first is a secondary market\072 if token holders can freely trade their stakes among other investors on an exchange\054 they can cash out without forcing a sale of the underlying property\056 The second is a drag\055along right3\051 embedded in the smart contract \024 a clause stipulating that if the majority owner decides to sell\054 minority token holders must sell on the same terms\056 This eliminates the risk of minority holders obstructing a transaction\056\n\nThere is also an equity question\056 From the token holders\047 perspective\054 the owner is effectively living rent\055free on their share of the asset\056 This can be resolved by treating the arrangement as an imputed rent structure\072 the owner pays token holders a monthly amount equivalent to their proportional share of market rent \024 either as an ongoing dividend or as a priority claim on proceeds at the time of sale\056 For instance\054 if 30 percent of the apartment has been tokenized\054 token holders would receive the equivalent of 30 percent of the prevailing monthly rent\056 The moment tokens generate a regular cash flow\054 they cease to be abstract digital entries and become a tangible financial instrument backed by a real asset\056)
     /K [ 2 ]
     /P 11373 0 R
     /Pg 3427 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

