11386 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The second scenario approaches the problem from the buyer\047s side\056 Ms\056 B wants to purchase an apartment unit worth 10 billion won \050approximately \0447\0562 million USD\051\054 but her total available funds \024 personal savings plus maximum borrowing \024 come to only 7 billion won\056 Rather than walking away from the purchase\054 she issues tokens representing a 30 percent stake in the apartment and raises the remaining 3 billion won \050roughly \0442\0562 million USD\051 from investors\056 The fundraising window opens after she pays a 10 percent deposit and runs through to the closing date\056\n\nWhat makes this model genuinely radical is that it redefines the nature of the financing itself \024 from debt to partnership\056 A conventional mortgage carries fixed monthly payments and the risk of forced auction if repayments fail\056 In the tokenized structure\054 investors are not Ms\056 B\047s creditors\073 they are co\055owners who share her belief in the apartment\047s appreciation\056 They receive the right to 30 percent of any future capital gain at the time of sale\054 but make no claim on     \052)
