11470 0 obj
  << /ActualText (In theory\054 the price of a real estate token should move in lockstep with the market value of the underlying apartment unit\056 In practice\054 a gap between the two can open up\056 Even if an apartment unit appreciates from 10 billion to 20 billion won\054 there is no guarantee that tokens representing a 30 percent stake \024 originally worth 3 billion won \024 will trade at exactly 6 billion won on the secondary market\056 The culprit is the illiquidity discount\056\n\nEven where a secondary market exists and tokens can be traded freely\054 ultimate cash realization depends on the apartment unit itself being sold\056 When a token holder\047s desired exit timeline diverges from the owner\047s willingness to sell\054 the token can become a stranded asset \024 its underlying value risen\054 but the path to realizing that value blocked\056 A market confronted with this mismatch will naturally price the token at a discount to its unrealized value\056)
     /K [ 3 ]
     /P 11471 0 R
     /Pg 5033 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

11471 0 obj
  << /K [ 11470 0 R ]
     /P 11482 0 R
     /S /P
     /Pg 5033 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

11472 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Three Lines of Defense)
     /K [ 4 ]
     /P 11473 0 R
     /Pg 5033 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

11473 0 obj
  << /K [ 11472 0 R ]
     /P 11482 0 R
     /S /P
     /Pg 5033 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

11474 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The first defense against price divergence is the imputed rent dividend \024 the mechanism discussed in Chapter 3\054 whereby the owner pays token holders a monthly cash amount equivalent to their proportional share of market rent\056 Regular cash distributions transform the token from a purely speculative instrument into one with an ongoing yield\054 narrowing the gap between its theoretical and market value\056\n\nThe second defense is a forced liquidation clause embedded in the smart contract\056 A provision stipulating that the apartment unit must be sold after\054 say\054 ten years \024 with capital gains distributed to token holders proportionally \024 or that the owner must buy out token holders at market value if no sale occurs\054 gives investors a defined exit horizon\056 Uncertainty about when\054 or whether\054 they will ever be made whole is one of the primary drivers of the illiquidity discount\056 A contractual endpoint removes it\056\n\nThe third defense is a periodic redemption right\072 a rule allowing investors to demand redemption at market value on a set date each year\054 with the owner obligated to purchase their tokens at that price\056 This effectively positions the owner as the buyer of last resort\054 providing an institutional guarantee of liquidity\056 The risk\054 however\054 is that this guarantee could collapse under pressure \024 a point that leads directly to the next section\056)
     /K [ 5 ]
     /P 11475 0 R
     /Pg 5033 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

