11506 0 obj
  << /ActualText (When most people first hear the idea of tokenizing a Gangnam apartment unit\054 their reaction is predictable\072 \042Is that even possible\077 There\047s no way the law allows it\056\042 It\047s a reasonable instinct\056 The token security structures examined in earlier chapters were themselves designed to navigate around potential conflicts with the existing legal framework \024 finding workable paths rather than confronting the system head\055on\056 The fundamental property of tokenization\054 after all\054 is fractional ownership\072 the effective transfer of real ownership rights to token holders in divided portions\056 That is precisely why any serious discussion of Gangnam apartment tokenization must confront a foundational question \024 whether fractional ownership of both the ownership title and the right of occupancy of an apartment unit is legally achievable\056 At that question\054 skepticism is not unreasonable\056\n\nThe skepticism has two roots\056 The first is the belief that the state cannot afford to leave the real estate market to operate as a purely free market\056 Property is a tax base\054 a political flashpoint\054 and a domain the state has always kept within its regulatory reach \024 not least because the free movement of capital through tokenization could\054 in the wrong hands\054 produce victims\056 The second is the conviction that the existing legal framework governing real estate property rights is simply too rigid to absorb something as novel as tokenization\056 Korean property law already recognizes a dizzying array of overlapping rights \024 ownership\054 possession\054 tenancy\054 jeonse\0544\051 liens \024 and adding fractional digital ownership on top of that structure is no small thing\056\n\nBoth concerns are legitimate\056 But examined against the longer arc of Korean real estate history\054 they begin to look less like permanent barriers and more like the familiar resistance that has preceded every major shift in how this market operates\056 The skepticism\054 in other words\054 may be reading the relationship between law and market as static \024 when the historical record suggests it is anything but\056)
     /K [ 4 ]
     /P 11507 0 R
     /Pg 5681 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

