11519 0 obj
  << /ActualText (investing law with an authority that borders on the sacred\056 Yet this familiar faith in legal completeness does not survive contact with reality\056 Korea\047s own property registration system \024 the bedrock of all real estate transactions in the country \024 is proof enough\056\n\nArticle 186 of the Civil Actxvi stipulates that any change in real property rights arising from a legal act must be registered to take effect\056 Registration is what triggers legal validity and brings ownership under the protection of the state\056 But \042takes effect\042 should not be read as \042is guaranteed by the state\056\042 Korea\047s property registration system does not carry absolute public trust \024 meaning that even a buyer who relies in good faith on the registered title bears the risk that the registration itself may be invalidated\056xvii If the underlying cause of a registration is found to be void\054 the parties must resolve the resulting ownership dispute through litigation\054 at their own cost\056 The state administers the register\073 it does not warrant its contents\056 The fact that even this state\055sanctioned institution contains such a significant gap \024 that the registered record may not reflect the truth \024 is itself an invitation to reconsider whether the current legal framework is truly the only reliable guarantor of real estate property rights\056)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 11520 0 R
     /Pg 6005 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
