11546 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Korean real estate history repeatedly demonstrates that markets arrive before the laws that govern them\056 The rules come later\054 drafted to manage and legitimize what the market has already built\056 Jeonse is the clearest example\056 It emerged not from legislation but from an organic private arrangement between landlords who needed a lump sum and tenants who preferred to avoid monthly rent \024 a credit\055based custom resting on personal trust rather than legal rights\056 Its precise origins are difficult to pin down\054 but what is certain is that it spread rapidly as a practical solution for absorbing the wave of migrants flooding into Seoul after the Korean War\056 The state eventually caught up\072 in 1958\054 Article 303 of the Civil Actxviii formally codified jeonse as a recognized legal right\056\n\nThe explosive growth of Seoul\047s population fed the formation of sprawling shanty towns\054 saddling the government with the problem of what to do with them\056 The Park Chung\055hee administration\054 preoccupied above all with post\055war security and the strategic risks of a massively overcrowded capital\054 responded with the \042South Seoul Plan\042 \024 the project that transformed what is now Gangnam from farmland into one of the world\047s most expensive urban districts\056 That transformation drew speculative capital like a magnet\056\n\nThe original residents of the Gangnam area slated for redevelopment were issued paper relocation certificates \024 official documents confirming their eligibility for resettlement housing\056 People folded these certificates and carried them in their pockets\054 and traded them between one another\054 and so the papers acquired their nickname\072 ddakji \024 a Korean word for a small folded paper card\0565\051 It was not long before buyers began paying a premium above the face value of these certificates\056 Some were genuine believers in Gangnam\047s future\073 others were simply inflating prices to sell on\056 The typical scheme worked like this\072 a speculator would buy a certificate from an original resident at a modest premium\054 then sell it to a third party at a larger one\056 When the time came for residents to take possession\054 title would first be registered in the original resident\047s name before being immediately transferred to the final buyer \024 a practice known as bokdeunggi\054 or double registration\056 These informal trading practices \024 ddakji voucher flipping and double registration \024 took root in the market well before the law had a chance to respond\054 and they generated the price bubbles that invariably followed\056 When the speculative frenzy became a social crisis\054 the government introduced the housing subscription systemxix through the National Housing Priority Supply Rules of 1977\054 extended the following year to private housing \024 an attempt to prioritize genuine end\055users over speculators from the point of initial sale\056\n\nLegislation\054 however\054 has never been able to permanently outwit the market\056 The subscription system created new rules\073 the market immediately found new gaps\056 The bokdeokbang \024 the traditional neighborhood property brokerages\054 whose name literally meant \042a room of fortune and virtue\054\042 run by respected local figures who accepted whatever commission a client could afford \024 were gradually corrupted by the speculative fever of Gangnam\047s development boom in the late 1970s\054 transforming into hubs for subscription\055right trading and premium dealing\056 Out of this milieu emerged the bokbuin \024 the so\055called \042fortune wives\054\042 a cohort of predominantly female property speculators who       \052)
     /K [ 5 ]
     /P 11547 0 R
     /Pg 6303 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

