11559 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The arc of human governance can be traced through three successive frameworks\072 theocracy \024 Rule of Gods \024 where authority flowed from divine mandate\073 monarchy \024 Rule of Kings \024 where it flowed from royal power\073 and constitutional democracy \024 Rule of Laws \024 where it flows from written statutes applied equally to all\056 \042All citizens are equal before the law\042 is the distilled essence of that third framework\056\n\nThe Rule of Law as a governing system was largely shaped by the Anglo\055American tradition\054 and the United States Constitution of the eighteenth century gave it its most durable institutional form \024 establishing the supremacy of written law and the principle of limited power\056 That constitutional framework became the operating system that made America a superpower and exported itself as the standard model for modern nation\055states worldwide\056\n\nYet the Rule of Law has always attracted critics as well as adherents\056 The most persistent objection is structural\072 access to legal remedies is not equal in practice\054 because access to legal services is tied to economic resources\056 The same law produces different outcomes depending on how much a party can spend on lawyers\056 The growing discussion of AI\055assisted adjudication \024 using algorithms to increase the consistency and predictability of rulings \024 is a response to precisely this recognized inadequacy\056\n\nA deeper structural critique concerns the tendency of Rule of Law systems to slide\054 in practice\054 into Rule by Law \024 where those who write and enforce the rules gradually weaken the legal constraints that apply to themselves\054 with limited internal mechanisms for correction\056 This is not a pathology specific to any one country or regime\073 it is an inherent vulnerability in the architecture of legal governance itself\056\n\nInto this long\055running debate\054 a genuinely new force has entered\056 Blockchain and artificial intelligence are creating pressure for a structural transformation that has been building for decades \024 a possible fourth stage of governance\072 the Rule of Codes\056 In this emerging order\054 contracts are not enforced by human judgment but by code\056 Smart contracts\054 decentralized finance\054 and AI agent\055based management are all early symptoms of this transition\056 For real estate tokenization\054 the implications are significant\056 The transparency of real\055time       \052)
     /K [ 4 ]
     /P 11560 0 R
     /Pg 6597 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

