11592 0 obj
  << /ActualText (won\047t allow it\054\042 are they describing a current institutional constraint \024 or declaring a permanent limit\077 Korean real estate history answers that question repeatedly and consistently\056 Jeonse rights\054 the subscription system\054 the real estate agent licensing regime\054 the real\055name ownership law \024 all were institutionalized as responses to markets that had already moved\056 There is no historical reason to expect real estate tokenization to be an exception\056 Moreover\054 the transparent ownership records and automatic enforcement mechanisms that smart contracts provide are a direct technological remedy for the very gap in the registration system\047s public trustworthiness identified in Section 5\0561\056 Technology moves first\073 institutions follow\056 That is the logic of the history we have been tracing \024 and there is no reason to expect real estate tokenization to be the exception\056\n\nAt the intersection of state\054 market\054 and technology\054 a new equilibrium is being assembled\056 And technology is not merely improving the existing road \024 it is laying an entirely different kind of infrastructure\056 At that junction\054 Gangnam apartment tokenization points beyond the workaround of token securities toward a horizon where property rights in their entirety \024 ownership title\054 right of occupancy\054 the full bundle \024 become tokenizable\056 The skeptics who say the law won\047t allow it are not wrong\056 At least not yet\056 But if history holds to its pattern \024 if the market moves first and the law follows \024 then not yet may become now sooner than anyone expects\056)
     /K [ 0 ]
